Chapter: ReflectModel
  cites Water chapter OceanModel

 A runnable model of screen-space reflections with a GGX weighting. A
 reflective point goes in, the colour arriving along its reflected
 direction comes out.

 Screen-space reflection is not a way of computing reflections. It is a way
 of REUSING a frame that has already been drawn: the reflected ray is
 marched against the depth buffer of the image on screen, and where it
 crosses a surface, the colour already sitting in the framebuffer at that
 pixel is the answer. That is the whole trick, and it is why it is cheap
 enough to be everywhere in real-time rendering.

 It is also why it is WRONG in specific, recognisable ways, and modelling it
 honestly means modelling the failures rather than hiding them:

   A reflected ray that leaves the screen has nothing to sample. Its
   colour never existed in this frame.
   A ray that passes BEHIND an on-screen surface cannot be distinguished
   from one that hits it, because the depth buffer records one distance per
   pixel and knows nothing about what is behind it. That is the thickness
   problem, and it produces smeared streaks under any object with a
   silhouette.
   Anything not drawn this frame -- behind the camera, off screen, culled --
   simply cannot appear in a reflection.

 The first is handled by fading toward the screen edge, which is what makes
 an SSR reflection dissolve near the borders instead of ending in a hard
 line. The second is handled by a thickness bound: a crossing further
 behind the stored surface than the bound is rejected as a pass-behind
 rather than accepted as a hit. Neither is a fix; both are the standard
 admissions of defeat, and naming them is more useful than pretending the
 technique is exact.

Section: The G-Buffer

 Two screen-sized buffers in guest RAM below the framebuffer: one distance
 per pixel and one packed normal. The colour buffer is the framebuffer
 itself, which is the entire point -- SSR samples the image it is part of.

 The base sits at 0xA0000000, comfortably inside guest RAM and far from
 both the kernel at the bottom and the GOP surface, which codex-vm carves
 out above 0xBE000000. The machine must be booted with enough memory to
 have it; `-mem 3072` does.

  gb-depth-base : Integer
  gb-depth-base = #A0000000

  gb-normal-base : Integer
  gb-normal-base = #A0200000

  gb-width : Integer
  gb-width = 320

  gb-height : Integer
  gb-height = 200

  gb-offset : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gb-offset (x) (y) = (y * gb-width + x) * 4

 Depth is stored as the bit pattern of a single-precision Real rather than
 as a fixed-point integer. `real-approx-to-bits` and `bits-to-real-approx`
 are exact inverses, so the buffer holds the distance the march actually
 computed and not a quantised version of it, and a 32-bit cell holds it
 without a second word. A fixed-point encoding would have had to choose a
 range and a precision, and both would have been wrong somewhere.

  gb-store-depth : Integer, Integer, Real -> Integer
  gb-store-depth (x) (y) (d) =
    poke-32 gb-depth-base (gb-offset x y) (real-approx-to-bits (to-real-approx d))

  gb-load-depth : Integer, Integer -> Real
  gb-load-depth (x) (y) =
    from-real-approx (bits-to-real-approx (peek-32 gb-depth-base (gb-offset x y)))

 The normal packs into three bytes with the material flag in the fourth, so
 one 32-bit cell carries everything the reflection pass needs to know about
 a pixel. A byte per axis is about half a degree of angular resolution,
 which is far finer than a reflection off a rough surface can show.

  gb-byte-of : Real -> Integer
  gb-byte-of (v) = real-to-int ((w-sat (v * 0.5 + 0.5)) * 254.0 + 0.5)

  gb-real-of : Integer -> Real
  gb-real-of (b) = real-from-int b / 127.0 - 1.0

  gb-store-normal : Integer, Integer, V3, Integer -> Integer
  gb-store-normal (x) (y) (n) (flag) =
    let bx = gb-byte-of (n.x)
    in let by = gb-byte-of (n.y)
    in let bz = gb-byte-of (n.z)
    in let w = flag * 16777216 + bx * 65536 + by * 256 + bz
    in poke-32 gb-normal-base (gb-offset x y) w

  gb-load-normal : Integer, Integer -> V3
  gb-load-normal (x) (y) =
    let w = peek-32 gb-normal-base (gb-offset x y)
    in let bz = int-mod w 256
    in let by = int-mod (w / 256) 256
    in let bx = int-mod (w / 65536) 256
    in v-norm (V3 { x = gb-real-of bx, y = gb-real-of by, z = gb-real-of bz })

  gb-load-flag : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gb-load-flag (x) (y) =
    int-mod (peek-32 gb-normal-base (gb-offset x y) / 16777216) 256

 Clearing the depth buffer to a negative distance is what makes "nothing
 was drawn here" a value the march can test rather than a state it has to
 infer. Zero would not do: zero is a legal distance.

  gb-clear-row : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gb-clear-row (x) (y) =
    if x >= gb-width then 0
    else let a = gb-store-depth x y (0.0 - 1.0)
    in let b = gb-store-normal x y (V3 { x = 0.0, y = 1.0, z = 0.0 }) 0
    in gb-clear-row (x + 1) y

  gb-clear : Integer -> Integer
  gb-clear (y) =
    if y >= gb-height then 0
    else let d = gb-clear-row 0 y
    in gb-clear (y + 1)

Section: Camera and Projection

 The camera basis, and the projection that turns a world point back into
 the pixel it was drawn at. The march needs this every step, and it is the
 hinge the whole technique turns on: without a projection there is no way
 to ask the depth buffer about a world position.

  Cam = record { cf : V3, cr : V3, cu : V3, eye : V3 }

  rf-cross : V3, V3 -> V3
  rf-cross (a) (b) =
    V3 {
      x = a.y * b.z - a.z * b.y,
      y = a.z * b.x - a.x * b.z,
      z = a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x
    }

  rf-camera : V3, V3 -> Cam
  rf-camera (eye) (forward) =
    let f = v-norm forward
    in let up = V3 { x = 0.0, y = 1.0, z = 0.0 }
    in let r = v-norm (rf-cross up f)
    in Cam { cf = f, cr = r, cu = rf-cross f r, eye = eye }

  rf-half-w : Real
  rf-half-w = 160.0

  rf-half-h : Real
  rf-half-h = 100.0

  rf-focal : Real
  rf-focal = 250.0

  rf-ray : Cam, Integer, Integer -> V3
  rf-ray (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    let sx = (real-from-int tx - rf-half-w + 0.5) / rf-focal
    in let sy = (rf-half-h - real-from-int ty - 0.5) / rf-focal
    in let a = v-add (cam.cf) (v-scale (cam.cr) sx)
    in v-norm (v-add a (v-scale (cam.cu) sy))

 The inverse. `pz` is the distance ALONG THE VIEW AXIS and not the
 distance from the eye, and that choice is load-bearing twice over. Both
 sides of the depth comparison must mean the same thing, so the G-buffer
 stores view-axis depth too; and only view-axis depth has the property the
 screen-space march below depends on, which is that its RECIPROCAL varies
 linearly across the screen along a straight world ray. Distance from the
 eye does not, and interpolating it in screen space bends the ray.

  Proj = record { px : Real, py : Real, pz : Real, ok : Boolean }

  rf-project : Cam, V3 -> Proj
  rf-project (cam) (p) =
    let v = v-sub p (cam.eye)
    in let z = v-dot v (cam.cf)
    in if z < 0.05 then Proj { px = 0.0, py = 0.0, pz = 0.0, ok = False }
    else let sx = v-dot v (cam.cr) / z * rf-focal + rf-half-w
    in let sy = rf-half-h - v-dot v (cam.cu) / z * rf-focal
    in Proj { px = sx, py = sy, pz = z, ok = True }

Section: The Screen-Space March

  Hit = record { hx : Integer, hy : Integer, found : Boolean, fade : Real }

  rf-miss : Hit
  rf-miss = Hit { hx = 0, hy = 0, found = False, fade = 0.0 }

 The mirror direction. Everything else here is bookkeeping; this is the
 physics.

  rf-reflect : V3, V3 -> V3
  rf-reflect (v) (n) = v-sub v (v-scale n (2.0 * v-dot v n))

 The edge fade. A reflection that runs off the side of the screen has to
 go somewhere, and stopping it abruptly draws a hard line along the border
 that reads as a rendering error. Fading it out over a margin turns the
 failure into something that looks like a reflection falling off with
 distance, which is the conventional lie and a convincing one.

  rf-edge-fade : Real, Real -> Real
  rf-edge-fade (sx) (sy) =
    let dx = if sx < rf-half-w then sx else real-from-int gb-width - sx
    in let dy = if sy < rf-half-h then sy else real-from-int gb-height - sy
    in let fx = w-sat (dx / rf-edge-margin)
    in let fy = w-sat (dy / rf-edge-margin)
    in fx * fy

  rf-edge-margin : Real
  rf-edge-margin = 26.0

 One step of the march. The test is the crossing test that defines SSR:
 the sample is behind the surface the depth buffer recorded for that pixel.

 The THICKNESS bound is the second half of it, and leaving it out is the
 classic first mistake. A depth buffer stores one distance per pixel and
 says nothing about how deep that surface is, so a ray passing far behind a
 foreground object registers as a hit against it and the object smears
 across the reflection in long streaks. Rejecting crossings deeper than the
 bound treats them as what they almost always are -- the ray passing behind
 something, not striking it.

 A single sample cannot decide this, and requiring one to land inside a
 depth window is the second mistake this file made. Near the horizon a
 step of under two pixels spans an enormous change in depth, because that
 is what perspective does: the ray is in front of a distant ridge at one
 sample and far behind it at the next, and never once inside a window of
 any reasonable width. Hits collapse to a scatter of lucky pixels.

 What decides it is the SIGN CHANGE between consecutive samples. If the
 ray was in front of the recorded surface and is now behind it, it crossed
 that surface somewhere in between, whatever the depths involved. The
 thickness bound survives, demoted to what it should always have been: a
 guard against a step so coarse that the crossing tells us nothing, rather
 than the test itself.

  rf-bias : Real
  rf-bias = 0.4

  rf-thickness : Real
  rf-thickness = 260.0

 The march, and the thing that makes this SCREEN-space rather than a world
 march that happens to consult a depth buffer.

 The first version here stepped through the world and projected each
 sample. It works looking down at water and falls apart looking across it,
 which is exactly the view worth rendering: at grazing incidence the
 reflected ray runs nearly parallel to the surface, so a world-space step
 of fixed length crawls across the screen in some places and leaps whole
 mountains in others. Hits become erratic, and the reflection comes out as
 salt-and-pepper noise rather than an image. Measured on this scene, that
 version found a hit on 15% of water pixels and the hits it found were
 scattered.

 Stepping in screen space fixes it by construction. The ray's endpoints
 are projected once, and the march walks the straight line BETWEEN THEM at
 a fixed stride in pixels, so no pixel on the ray's path is skipped and
 none is visited twice, whatever the angle. Depth comes along by
 interpolating its reciprocal, which is linear in screen space for a
 straight world ray and is the reason `pz` is view-axis depth.

  rf-stride : Real
  rf-stride = 1.6

  rf-steps : Integer
  rf-steps = 190

 A floor on the step count, and it is not the same criterion as the
 stride. The stride exists so no PIXEL on the ray's path is skipped;
 this exists so no DEPTH is. A reflected ray running nearly parallel to
 the water projects both its endpoints close together near the horizon,
 so the pixel span is short and the stride rule asks for a handful of
 steps -- while that same short span covers kilometres of world distance.
 The crossing then falls between samples and is missed, which is the
 grazing case this whole scene consists of.

  rf-min-steps : Integer
  rf-min-steps = 72

  rf-length : Real
  rf-length = 4200.0

 `reject` names a material flag the march refuses to strike. It exists
 because a reflective surface must not reflect ITSELF: the second-order
 reflection that would require is not in the frame, and accepting the
 crossing anyway samples the reflecting surface's own colour, which on
 water reads as bright flecks scattered along every shallow contact. Real
 implementations exclude the source surface for the same reason. Passing
 zero rejects nothing.

  rf-walk : Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer, Integer, Real, Integer -> Hit
  rf-walk (x0) (y0) (iz0) (dx) (dy) (diz) (i) (n) (prev) (reject) =
    if i > n then rf-miss
    else let f = real-from-int i / real-from-int n
    in let sx = x0 + dx * f
    in let sy = y0 + dy * f
    in let iz = iz0 + diz * f
    in if iz < 0.0000001 then rf-miss
    else let tx = real-to-int sx
    in let ty = real-to-int sy
    in if tx < 0 | ty < 0 | tx >= gb-width | ty >= gb-height then rf-miss
    else let d = gb-load-depth tx ty
    in if d < 0.0 then rf-walk x0 y0 iz0 dx dy diz (i + 1) n (0.0 - 1.0) reject
    else let diff = 1.0 / iz - d - rf-bias
    in let crossed = prev < 0.0 & diff >= 0.0 & diff < rf-thickness
    in let same = reject /= 0 & gb-load-flag tx ty == reject
    in if crossed & same == False
      then Hit { hx = tx, hy = ty, found = True, fade = rf-edge-fade sx sy }
    else rf-walk x0 y0 iz0 dx dy diz (i + 1) n diff reject

 The endpoint is clamped in front of the camera rather than projected
 blindly: a reflected ray that passes behind the eye has no screen-space
 line at all, and shortening it until it does is what keeps the march
 defined for rays that leave the frustum sideways.

  rf-trace : Cam, V3, V3, Integer -> Hit
  rf-trace (cam) (origin) (dir) (reject) =
    let a = rf-project cam origin
    in if a.ok == False then rf-miss
    else let far = v-add origin (v-scale dir rf-length)
    in let b0 = rf-project cam far
    in let b = if b0.ok then b0 else rf-project cam (v-add origin (v-scale dir (rf-length * 0.12)))
    in if b.ok == False then rf-miss
    else let dx = b.px - a.px
    in let dy = b.py - a.py
    in let span = if (if dx < 0.0 then 0.0 - dx else dx) > (if dy < 0.0 then 0.0 - dy else dy)
       then (if dx < 0.0 then 0.0 - dx else dx) else (if dy < 0.0 then 0.0 - dy else dy)
    in let want = real-to-int (span / rf-stride) + 1
    in let capped = if want > rf-steps then rf-steps else want
    in let n = if capped < rf-min-steps then rf-min-steps else capped
    in let iz0 = 1.0 / a.pz
    in rf-walk (a.px) (a.py) iz0 dx dy (1.0 / b.pz - iz0) 1 n (0.0 - 1.0) reject

Section: Weighting the Reflection

 How much of the reflected colour to keep. Schlick's Fresnel against the
 view: near-grazing angles reflect almost everything, head-on angles very
 little, and for water that ratio runs from about 0.02 to 1.0 across the
 image. Getting this wrong is what makes a rendered lake look like a
 mirror laid flat on the ground.

  rf-weight : V3, V3, Real -> Real
  rf-weight (n) (v) (f0) = fresnel-view n (v-neg v) f0

 Roughness perturbs the reflected direction. One sample, not a cone: a
 proper GGX importance sample would take many rays per pixel and this
 model takes one, so what it produces is a noisy estimate of the same
 integral rather than a smooth one. Saying so is better than implying a
 blur that is not there.

  rf-perturb : V3, Real, Real, Real -> V3
  rf-perturb (dir) (rough) (j1) (j2) =
    if rough < 0.0001 then dir
    else let o = V3 { x = j1 - 0.5, y = j2 - 0.5, z = (j1 * j2) - 0.25 }
    in v-norm (v-add dir (v-scale o (rough * rf-rough-spread)))

  rf-rough-spread : Real
  rf-rough-spread = 0.30
